15:All  the  subjects  were  normal  controls  and  were  obtained  from  the  Cambridge  Centre  for  Ageing  and Neuroscience (Cam-CAN, www.cam-can.org). The Cam-CAN is a large-scale collaborative research project at the University of Cambridge, launched in October 2010. This project used epidemiological, cognitive, and neuroimaging data to understand how individuals can best retain cognitive abilities into old age. A detailed description of the dataset and pre-processing pipeline can be found in (1). A more general overview of the Cam-CAN dataset can be found in (2). Demographic information can be seen in Table S1.
57:The NC patients were required to meet the following research criteria: (a) no memory concerns; (b) MMSE and MoCA scores within the normal range (adjusted for age, sex, and education); and (c) a CDR score of 0.
75:Subjects with MCI from Dataset 3 were obtained from the Alzheimer's Disease Neuroimaging Initiative (ADNI) database (http://adni.loni.usc.edu). The ADNI, launched in 2003 as a public-private partnership, and is a longitudinal natural history study whose primary purpose is informing the design of therapeutic trials in AD. The detailed inclusion criteria for the different diagnostic categories are available on the ADNI website (http://adni.loni.usc.edu/methods/documents/) (29-33). The demographic information sees Table S1.
105:Table S1. Demographic and neuropsychological data for all datasets.
